3. RestTemplate vs WebClient vs FeignClient in short

RestTemplate
- Blocking
- Working: Thread sends request, Thread waits(blocked), Response comes - Thread resumes
- Simple and predictable but doesn't scale well under high concurrency.
- Thread usage: one thread per request	
- Streaming: Limited

WebClient:
- Non blocking, event driven (reactive)
- Working: Request is sent, Thread is freed immediately, Response handled later via callback/reactive pipeline.
- Efficient for many concurrent calls
- Requires understanding reactive programming.
- Thread usage: Few threads handle many requests
- Streaming: Excellent

WebClient vs FeignClient
Feign: Declare an interface and it just works
WebClient: You build the http call step by step

Feign: it is declarative, minimal code
WebClient: programmatic, flexible

When to use Feign:
- When you want clean, readable code
- Your app is Spring MVC (blocking)
- Calls are simple CRUD style
- Teams prefers simplicity over control
- Very common in typical microservices

When to use Web Client:
- When you need high performance/scalability
- You are using reactive programming
- You need streaming
- You want fine control over headers, retries, filters 
